Selected Idea
-------------

Project Gutenberg's licensing paradox: public-domain texts free to use - with trademark and redistribution constraints
Although many Project Gutenberg eBooks are public-domain works that anyone in the U.S. can copy or reuse, the Project Gutenberg License and trademark rules impose specific obligations (keep the license, display a required sentence, and restrict use of the Project Gutenberg brand), creating a practical tension between unrestricted public-domain reuse and legal/brand constraints on redistribution.

Research
--------

### Facts

1. "This eBook is for the use of anyone anywhere in the United States and most other parts of the world at no cost and with almost no restrictions whatsoever." (opening text)

2. "You may copy it, give it away or re-use it under the terms of the Project Gutenberg License included with this eBook or online at www.gutenberg.org." (opening text)

3. "If you are not located in the United States, you will have to check the laws of the country where you are located before using this eBook." (opening text)

4. "Creating the works from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright law means that no one owns a United States copyright in these works, so the Foundation (and you!) can copy and distribute it in the United States without permission and without paying copyright royalties." (license text)

5. "Special rules, set forth in the General Terms of Use part of this license, apply to copying and distributing Project GutenbergT electronic works to protect the PROJECT GUTENBERGT concept and trademark." (license text)

6. "Project Gutenberg" is a registered trademark. (paragraph 1.B)

7. "The Project Gutenberg Literary Archive Foundation ('the Foundation' or PGLAF), owns a compilation copyright in the collection of Project Gutenberg electronic works." (paragraph 1.C)

8. "Nearly all the individual works in the collection are in the public domain in the United States." (paragraph 1.C)

9. "If an individual work is unprotected by copyright law in the United States and you are located in the United States, we do not claim a right to prevent you from copying, distributing, performing, displaying or creating derivative works based on the work as long as all references to Project Gutenberg are removed." (paragraph 1.C)

10. Paragraph 1.E.1 requires that a specified sentence, with active links to or other immediate access to the full Project Gutenberg License, "must appear prominently whenever any copy of a Project Gutenberg work (any work on which the phrase 'Project Gutenberg' appears, or with which the phrase 'Project Gutenberg' is associated) is accessed, displayed, performed, viewed, copied or distributed." (paragraph 1.E.1)

11. "Do not unlink or detach or remove the full Project Gutenberg License terms from this work, or any files containing a part of this work or any other work associated with Project Gutenberg." (paragraph 1.E.4)

12. "Do not copy, display, perform, distribute or redistribute this electronic work, or any part of this electronic work, without prominently displaying the sentence set forth in paragraph 1.E.1 with active links or immediate access to the full terms of the Project Gutenberg License." (paragraph 1.E.5)

13. "You may convert to and distribute this work in any binary, compressed, marked up, nonproprietary or proprietary form, including any word processing or hypertext form." (paragraph 1.E.6, first sentence)

14. Paragraph 1.E.6 further states that if you provide access to or distribute copies in a format other than the official "Plain Vanilla ASCII" or other format used in the official version, you must, at no additional cost, provide a copy, a means of exporting a copy, or a means of obtaining a copy upon request of the work in its original "Plain Vanilla ASCII" or other form, and any alternate format must include the full Project Gutenberg License as specified in paragraph 1.E.1. (paragraph 1.E.6)

15. "Do not charge a fee for access to, viewing, displaying, performing, copying or distributing any Project Gutenberg works unless you comply with paragraph 1.E.8 or 1.E.9." (paragraph 1.E.7)

16. Paragraph 1.E.8 states that you may charge a reasonable fee for copies or providing access provided that, among other listed conditions, you pay a royalty fee of 20% of the gross profits you derive from the use of Project Gutenberg works to the owner of the Project Gutenberg trademark and comply with other listed requirements. (paragraph 1.E.8)

17. "If an individual Project Gutenberg electronic work is posted with the permission of the copyright holder, your use and distribution must comply with both paragraphs 1.E.1 through 1.E.7 and any additional terms imposed by the copyright holder." (paragraph 1.E.3)

18. "If you wish to charge a fee or distribute a Project Gutenberg electronic work or group of works on different terms than are set forth in this agreement, you must obtain permission in writing from the Project Gutenberg Literary Archive Foundation." (paragraph 1.E.9)

19. "Project Gutenberg volunteers and employees expend considerable effort to identify, do copyright research on, transcribe and proofread works not protected by U.S. copyright law in creating the Project GutenbergT collection." (paragraph 1.F.1)

20. Project Gutenberg electronic works "may contain 'Defects,' such as, but not limited to, incomplete, inaccurate or corrupt data, transcription errors, a copyright or other intellectual property infringement, a defective or damaged disk or other medium, a computer virus, or computer codes that damage or cannot be read by your equipment." (paragraph 1.F.1)

21. Except for the limited right of replacement or refund, the Foundation and the trademark owner disclaim all liability for damages, costs and expenses, including legal fees. (paragraph 1.F.2)

22. Section 2 states that Project Gutenberg is synonymous with the free distribution of electronic works in formats readable by the widest variety of computers. (Section 2)

23. Section 3 identifies the Project Gutenberg Literary Archive Foundation as a non-profit 501(c)(3) educational corporation organized under Mississippi law and granted tax-exempt status by the IRS. (Section 3)

24. The opening license text states "You may use this eBook for nearly any purpose such as creation of derivative works, reports, performances and research." (opening license text)

25. "Project Gutenberg eBooks may be modified and printed and given away-you may do practically ANYTHING in the United States with eBooks not protected by U.S. copyright law." (license text)

26. "Redistribution is subject to the trademark license, especially commercial redistribution." (license text)


### Interpretations

1. The license requires redistributors to include or preserve Project Gutenberg's license text and to prominently display a specified sentence with links to the full license when copies or access to Project Gutenberg works are provided. (supported by Facts 2, 10, 11, 12, 14)

2. The license identifies "Project Gutenberg" as a registered trademark and states that redistribution is subject to trademark-related rules in the license. (supported by Facts 5, 6, 26, 18)

3. The license states both that nearly all individual works in the collection are public domain in the United States and that, for works unprotected by U.S. copyright, copying and distribution in the United States are permitted if references to Project Gutenberg are removed. (supported by Facts 8 and 9)


### Unknowns

1. Whether and how the Project Gutenberg trademark or license provisions are enforced against third parties who redistribute public-domain texts. (not established by the source)

2. The legal effect, in specific jurisdictions outside the United States, of Project Gutenberg's license and trademark restrictions on redistributing works that are public domain in the United States. (not established by the source)

3. The specific actions that satisfy the license condition "all references to Project Gutenberg are removed" (for example, whether removal from metadata, front matter, or visible text is sufficient). (not established by the source)

4. Whether removal of all references to Project Gutenberg is the only method the license contemplates for redistributing public-domain works without being subject to other license terms. (not established by the source)

5. How the Foundation interprets or applies the license requirement to "prominently" display the sentence required by paragraph 1.E.1 in a wide variety of distribution contexts (print, web, packaged ebooks, archived files). (not established by the source)

6. Whether retaining the full Project Gutenberg License in redistributed copies is legally required in jurisdictions other than the United States. (not established by the source)

7. Any examples of disputes, litigation, or formal challenges concerning the application of these license or trademark requirements. (not established by the source)


### Development sequence

1. The Foundation states that works created from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright law result in no U.S. copyright for those works. (Fact 4)

2. The Foundation states it owns a compilation copyright in the Project Gutenberg collection. (Fact 7)

Relationship between 1 and 2: UNKNOWN (the source does not state a causal or temporal relationship between compilation copyright ownership and the creation-from-print-editions statement).

3. The eBook includes license text saying the eBook may be copied, given away, or re-used under the Project Gutenberg License. (Fact 2)

Relationship between 2 and 3: UNKNOWN (the source does not explicitly link the compilation copyright statement to the inclusion of the license in individual eBooks).

4. The license text lists permitted uses and broad statements about free distribution and uses such as creating derivative works. (Facts 24, 25)

Relationship between 3 and 4: UNKNOWN (the source presents both but does not state a causal relationship).

5. The license sets out specific constraints and requirements for redistribution and formatting, including the requirement to prominently display a specified sentence linking to the full license, not removing the full license, format-conversion conditions, and restrictions on charging fees except under specified conditions. (Facts 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15)

Relationship between 4 and 5: UNKNOWN (the source presents the broad permissions and the specific constraints but does not state a causal relationship).

6. The license expressly identifies Project Gutenberg as a registered trademark and states redistribution is subject to the trademark license. (Facts 5, 6, 26)

Relationship between 5 and 6: UNKNOWN (the source presents both license obligations and trademark statements but does not state a causal link beyond placing them in the license).

7. The license provides a path for charging fees under detailed conditions including a 20% royalty and other requirements. (Fact 16)

Relationship between 6 and 7: UNKNOWN.


